      "rev_n_1_9f47b8_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_9f47b8_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_9f47b8",
        "thought_text": "Thought-1: The current greedy approach from both ends fails because it prematurely terminates when neither the leftmost nor rightmost element can be added to the increasing sequence. This oversight causes the algorithm to miss potential longer increasing subsequences. To address this, we need to modify the approach to continue checking both ends until all elements are processed, even if one end becomes exhausted. This ensures we find the longest possible increasing subsequence.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_9f47b8_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_cc150aefbe",
        "created_at": 1794511563.2058966
      },
      "rev_n_2_13713c_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_13713c_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_13713c",
        "thought_text": "The error in the previous solution was in the reconstruction phase. The code was only considering the left part of the sequence and not properly handling the transition to the right part. To fix this, we need to modify the reconstruction logic to correctly handle both left and right parts of the sequence.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_13713c_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_85748a7c7d",
        "created_at": 1794511589.2601025
      },
      "rev_n_3_1da68a_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_1da68a_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_1da68a",
        "thought_text": "Thought-1: The current implementation exits the loop one element early due to the condition 'left <= right'. This can miss the last element if it's part of the longest increasing sequence. To fix this, we should change the loop condition to 'left < right' and handle the last element separately after the loop.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_1da68a_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_0aae669763",
        "created_at": 1794511610.4711523
      },
      "rev_n_4_8df28c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_8df28c_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_8df28c",
        "thought_text": "Implement a dynamic programming approach to find the longest increasing subsequence (LIS) in the given array. This will allow us to determine the maximum length of the increasing sequence and reconstruct the moves to achieve it.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7685739344919582246",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794511626.606903
      },
      "rev_n_5_03c6b9_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_03c6b9_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_03c6b9",
        "thought_text": "Use a two-pointer approach to process elements from both ends of the array simultaneously. Keep track of the smallest and largest elements that can be added to the increasing sequence, updating them as we process elements.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7685739344919582246",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794511626.6069188
      },
      "rev_n_6_07f4ec_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_07f4ec_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_07f4ec",
        "thought_text": "Implement a greedy algorithm that always chooses the smaller of the leftmost or rightmost elements, but with a twist: if both elements can be added to the increasing sequence, choose the one that leads to a longer overall sequence in subsequent moves.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7685739344919582246",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794511626.606928
      },
      "rev_n_4_8df28c_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_8df28c_1",
        "node_id": "n_4_8df28c",
        "thought_text": "The current approach using dynamic programming to find the longest increasing subsequence (LIS) is correct, but the reconstruction step is flawed. Instead of stopping prematurely at the best_index, we should continue reconstructing the sequence from both ends until all elements are processed. This ensures we find the longest possible increasing subsequence and correctly reconstruct the moves to achieve it.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7685739344919582246",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_4_8df28c_0",
